1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,760
Right, everyone, let's make a start.

2
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:05,980
Over the past few sessions

3
00:00:05,980 --> 00:00:11,008
we've been considering the reasons why some world languages are in decline,

4
00:00:11,008 --> 00:00:15,840
and today I'm going to introduce another factor that affects languages,

5
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:17,140
and the speakers of those languages

6
00:00:17,140 --> 00:00:21,880
and that's technology and, in particular, digital technology.

7
00:00:21,880 --> 00:00:28,176
In order to illustrate its effect, I'm going to focus on the Icelandic language,

8
00:00:28,176 --> 00:00:32,080
which is spoken by around 321,000 people

9
00:00:32,260 --> 00:00:36,990
most of whom live in Iceland - an island in the North Atlantic Ocean.

10
00:00:36,990 --> 00:00:40,760
The problem for this language is not the number of speakers

11
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:42,880
even though this number is small.

12
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:47,240
Nor is it about losing words to other languages, such as English.

13
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:51,045
In fact, the vocabulary of Icelandic is continually increasing

14
00:00:51,045 --> 00:00:54,850
because when speakers need a new word for something,

15
00:00:54,850 --> 00:00:59,500
they tend to create one, rather than borrowing from another language.

16
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:03,060
All this makes Icelandic quite a special language

17
00:01:03,780 --> 00:01:06,608
it's changed very little in the past millennium,

18
00:01:06,608 --> 00:01:12,880
yet it can handle twenty-first-century concepts related to the use of computers and digital technology.

19
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:18,372
Take, for example, the word for web browser... this is vafri in Icelandic,

20
00:01:18,372 --> 00:01:21,330
which comes from the verb 'to wander'.

21
00:01:21,330 --> 00:01:23,903
I can't think of a more appropriate term

22
00:01:23,903 --> 00:01:27,620
because that's exactly what you do mentally when you browse the internet.

23
00:01:27,620 --> 00:01:30,880
Then there's an Icelandic word for podcast

24
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:34,060
which is too hard to pronounce! And so on.

25
00:01:34,060 --> 00:01:38,887
Icelandic, then, is alive and growing, but - and it's a big but -

26
00:01:38,887 --> 00:01:42,680
young Icelanders spend a great deal of time in the digital world

27
00:01:42,680 --> 00:01:45,570
and this world is predominantly English.

28
00:01:45,570 --> 00:01:48,240
Think about smartphones.

29
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:51,352
They didn't even exist until comparatively recently,

30
00:01:51,352 --> 00:01:56,021
but today young people use them all the time to read books,

31
00:01:56,021 --> 00:02:00,690
watch TV or films, play games, listen to music, and so on.

32
00:02:00,690 --> 00:02:04,180
Obviously, this is a good thing in many respects

33
00:02:04,180 --> 00:02:06,082
because it promotes their bilingual skills,

34
00:02:06,082 --> 00:02:11,114
but the extent of the influence of English in the virtual world is staggering

35
00:02:11,114 --> 00:02:13,630
and it's all happening really fast.

36
00:02:13,630 --> 00:02:15,743
For their parents and grandparents,

37
00:02:15,743 --> 00:02:18,080
the change is less concerning

38
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:21,660
because they already have their native-speaker skills in Icelandic.

39
00:02:21,660 --> 00:02:26,790
But for young speakers - well, the outcome is a little troubling.

40
00:02:26,790 --> 00:02:33,902
For example, teachers have found that playground conversations in Icelandic secondary schools can be conducted entirely in English,

41
00:02:33,902 --> 00:02:38,594
while teachers of much younger children have reported situations

42
00:02:39,035 --> 00:02:44,570
where their classes find it easier to say what is in a picture using English, rather than Icelandic

43
00:02:44,570 --> 00:02:56,790
The very real and worrying consequence of all this is that the young generation in Iceland is at risk of losing its mother tongue.

44
00:02:56,790 --> 00:03:00,395
Of course, this is happening to other European languages too,

45
00:03:00,395 --> 00:03:05,804
but while internet companies might be willing to offer, say, French options in their systems,

46
00:03:05,804 --> 00:03:16,620
it's much harder for them to justify the expense of doing the same for a language that has a population the size of a French town, such as Nice.

47
00:03:16,620 --> 00:03:20,000
The other drawback of Icelandic is the grammar

48
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:24,020
which is significantly more complex than in most languages.

49
00:03:24,020 --> 00:03:29,090
At the moment, the tech giants are simply not interested in tackling this.

50
00:03:29,090 --> 00:03:33,360
So, what is the Icelandic government doing about this?

51
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:42,647
Well, large sums of money are being allocated to a language technology fund that it is hoped will lead to the development of Icelandic sourced apps

52
00:03:42,647 --> 00:03:45,080
and other social media and digital systems

53
00:03:45,080 --> 00:03:48,720
but clearly this is going to be an uphill struggle.

54
00:03:48,720 --> 00:03:55,480
On the positive side, they know that Icelandic is still the official language of education and government.

55
00:03:55,480 --> 00:03:58,617
It has survived for well over a thousand years

56
00:03:58,617 --> 00:04:03,160
and the experts predict that its future in this nation state is sound

57
00:04:03,160 --> 00:04:05,240
and will continue to be so.

58
00:04:05,240 --> 00:04:11,880
However, there's no doubt that it's becoming an inevitable second choice in young people's lives.

59
00:04:11,880 --> 00:04:15,140
This raises important questions.

60
00:04:15,140 --> 00:04:19,468
When you consider how much of the past is tied up in a language,

61
00:04:19,468 --> 00:04:22,560
will young Icelanders lose their sense of their own identity?

62
00:04:22,560 --> 00:04:26,430
Another issue that concerns the government of Iceland is this.

63
00:04:26,430 --> 00:04:29,875
If children are learning two languages through different routes,

64
00:04:29,875 --> 00:04:33,200
neither of which they are fully fluent in

65
00:04:33,200 --> 00:04:36,000
will they be able to express themselves properly?
